    "q-131269": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131269",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "At a dental clinic, patients arrive at an average rate of $10$ patients every hour during the hours when the clinic is open. What is the probability that exactly $5$ customers will arrive in the next hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that patients arrive independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients who will arrive in the next hour. Since the average rate is $10$ patients every hour when the clinic is open, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of patients can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =10,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(10).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{10}^{x}{e}^{-10}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly $5$ customers will arrive in the next hour while the clinic is open is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{10}^{5}{e}^{-10}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
