    "q-85329": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85329",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "It is known that, on average, $6$ cell phones per batch produced by a cellphone factory have defects. What is the probability that the next batch contains exactly $4$ phones with defects? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective phones occur independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cell phones with defects. Since the average rate is $6$ cell phones per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, $X$ can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having $4$ cell phones with defects in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{4}{e}^{-6}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.134\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
