    "q-85384": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85384",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "A bank manager notices that, on average, for each group of  customers, $9$ of them visit the cash machine. What is the probability that in the next group of customers, less than $5$ customers visit the cash machine? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that visits of cash machine occur independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that visit the cash machine. Since the average rate is $9$ customers per group, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of customers that visit the cash machine can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =9,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(9).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{9}^{x}{e}^{-9}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that less than $5$ customers visit the cash machine in the next group is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{0}{e}^{-9}}{0!}+\\frac{{9}^{1}{e}^{-9}}{1!}+\\frac{{9}^{2}{e}^{-9}}{2!}+\\frac{{9}^{3}{e}^{-9}}{3!}+\\frac{{9}^{4}{e}^{-9}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.055\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
