    "q-85375": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85375",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "The hospital emergency room receives an average of $7$ patients per hour. What is the probability that it receives fewer than $4$ patients in the next hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that patients arrive independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients in the hospital emergency room. Since the average rate is $7$ patients per hour, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of patients can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having fewer than $4$ patients in the next hour is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!}+\\frac{{7}^{3}{e}^{-7}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.082\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
