      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "In a certain University, professors report that, on average, out of each group of graduate students, $5$ students graduate with the Summa Cum Laude honor. What is the probability that in the next group of graduate students, no more than $3$ students will have the Summa Cum Laude honor? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that receiving the Summa Cum Laude honor occurs independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of graduate students with the Summa Cum Laude honor. Since the average rate is $5$ graduates with the Summa Cum Laude honor of each group of graduates, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of graduate students can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that at most $3$ graduate students will have the Summa Cum Laude honor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{0}{e}^{-5}}{0!}+\\frac{{5}^{1}{e}^{-5}}{1!}+\\frac{{5}^{2}{e}^{-5}}{2!}+\\frac{{5}^{3}{e}^{-5}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
