    "q-89244": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89244",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "On average, a wedding dress store sells $9$ wedding dresses per week. What is the probability that the wedding dress store will sell more than $3$ wedding dresses next week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that wedding dress sales occur independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of wedding dresses sold. Since the average rate is $9$ wedding dresses sold per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of wedding dresses sold can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =9,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(9).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{9}^{x}{e}^{-9}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{0}{e}^{-9}}{0!}+\\frac{{9}^{1}{e}^{-9}}{1!}+\\frac{{9}^{2}{e}^{-9}}{2!}+\\frac{{9}^{3}{e}^{-9}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.021\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of selling more than $3$ wedding dresses next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.021 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
