    "q-131431": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131431",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "The professor of geometry notices that, on average, every batch of practice questions contains $6$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution. What is the probability that in the next batch of practice questions, there will be more than $5$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution occur independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution. Since the average rate is $6$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>5)=1-P(X\\le 5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}{e}^{-6}}{0!}+\\frac{{6}^{1}{e}^{-6}}{1!}+\\frac{{6}^{2}{e}^{-6}}{2!}+\\frac{{6}^{3}{e}^{-6}}{3!}+\\frac{{6}^{4}{e}^{-6}}{4!}+\\frac{{6}^{5}{e}^{-6}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.446\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $5$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution in the next batch of practice questions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.446 \\\\  & =0.554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
